ck at the task requirements, I need to add R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 traceability to main.go even though R4 is about testing, and ensure the error diagnostics and flag handling are working correctly in the implementation. the \"functionality\" that R4 tests verify)\n2. mkdir_test.go: Add differential test cases\n\nFor main.go, the existing code probably already works correctly. But the traceability comment should be updated.\n\nFor mkdir_test.go, I should add any missing test cases. Let me add:\n\n1. Test for invalid short option (`-x`)\n2. Test for `--help` (unit test - check it produces output and exits 0)\n3. Test for `--version` (unit test - check it produces output and exits 0)\n4. Test for `--` separator (differential test)\n\nActually, for --help and --version, we can't do a full differential test because the content differs. But we can verify they produce non-empty stdout and exit 0. This is a unit test, not a diff test.\n\nBut wait - the task is about R4 which is specifically about differential testing. So maybe I should only add differential tests.\n\nI'm realizing that --help and --version outputs can't be fully differential-tested since the content varies, but I can still verify they work by checking exit codes and output presence. Looking at the broader scope of the task around error diagnostics and version/help functionality, I should add differential tests for error message formats while also including tests for --help and --version that at least confirm they execute properly. Let me update the main.go traceability comment to reference R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3, then add the missing test cases to mkdir_test.go covering the invalid short option, help and version flags, and the -- separator. Writing unit tests... I'm examining the error handling flow in main.go to see if the \"Try 'mkdir --help'\" suggestion is already being printed on parse errors, which would match gmkdir's behavior and inform what my tests need to verify. So the \"Try...\" message only appears