t` is the right approach since it's invalid for both implementations. Now I'll finalize the implementation by updating the traceability comment in main.go to reference the relevant requirements. I need to verify each test will actually pass by checking the output matches between our implementation and gmkdir. For the invalid option test, the error messages align after normalization. For help and version tests, I can't compare stdout directly since ours differs from gmkdir's, so I'll just verify both exit with code 0 and have empty stderr.\n\nWriting test code... Writing test functions... Writing test subtests...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the permission test coverage—I have tests for no flags, -m alone, -m with -p, and -p alone, so I need to verify I'm covering the -p without -m case to complete the matrix.\n\nActually, adding more mode variations with -p would be scope creep since the requirement just asks for the combination of -m and -p flags to be tested, not every possible mode value, so the existing test is sufficient.\n\nLet me move forward with implementation and double-check the function size constraints—the helper functions are well under any limits, though I should verify the test function itself doesn't exceed the 40-line hard limit. I need to add those remaining requirement references to the test file comment and then implement the new subtests for invalid options, help flag, version flag, and the double-dash separator case.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering whether the test file comment should reference R1.1-R1.4 at all. The existing pattern shows that the comment lists what the tests verify, and since R4 is about testing itself, adding R1 references might be redundant when those requirements are primarily implemented in main.go. I'll keep the test file comment focused on what it actually tests and add R4 to the main.go comment instead, since the functionality there enables those tests to pass.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"s